{"id":"gen-1790979451-3YYKhaY42j1jbyH5GKQo","object":"chat.completion","created":1790979451,"model":"cohere/north-mini-code:free","provider":"Cohere","system_fingerprint":null,"service_tier":null,"choices":[{"index":0,"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop","native_finish_reason":"complete","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"intent\": \"CONCLUDE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6582-raw-integrator-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"conclusion\": {\n      \"assignment\": {\n        \"a\": 3,\n        \"b\": 2,\n        \"c\": 1,\n        \"d\": 2,\n        \"e\": 0,\n        \"f\": 1,\n        \"g\": 3,\n        \"h\": 2,\n        \"i\": 0,\n        \"j\": 3,\n        \"k\": 0,\n        \"l\": 2,\n        \"m\": 0,\n        \"n\": 1,\n        \"o\": 3,\n        \"p\": 2\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6582\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6582\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"raw-integrator\"\n}","refusal":null,"reasoning":"We need to solve the CSP. Let's restate:\n\nWe have variables a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p each domain {0,1,2,3}. There are constraints c1..c16 for pairs of variables, and also c17..c20 bridging constraints between components: c17 is between b and e, allowed pairs: [2,0] and [0,2]; c18 between f and i allowed [1,0] and [2,1]; c19 between j and m allowed [3,0] and [0,0]; c20 between n and a allowed [1,3] and [1,0].\n\nWe need to find a complete assignment satisfying all constraints.\n\nConstraints c1..c16 are local constraints for adjacent variables in a cycle of components: c1: a,b; c2: b,c; c3: c,d; c4: d,a; c5: e,f; c6: f,g; c7: g,h; c8: h,e; c9: i,j; c10: j,k; c11: k,l; c12: l,i; c13: m,n; c14: n,o; c15: o,p; c16: p,m. So there are two independent cycles: (a,b,c,d) and (e,f,g,h) and (i,j,k,l) and (m,n,o,p). Actually each of those four cycles is separate: c1-c4 is a cycle of a,b,c,d; c5-c8 is cycle of e,f,g,h; c9-c12 is cycle of i,j,k,l; c13-c16 is cycle of m,n,o,p. So we need to assign each cycle satisfying its local constraints and also bridging constraints across cycles: c17 connects b (from cycle1) to e (cycle2). c18 connects f (cycle2) to i (cycle3). c19 connects j (cycle3) to m (cycle4). c20 connects n (cycle4) to a (cycle1). So overall it's a larger cycle linking all four cycles.\n\nWe need to find any assignment (maybe unique) that satisfies all constraints.\n\nApproach: brute force search manually? There are 4^16 = 4,294,967,296 possibilities, too many. But we can reason.\n\nLet's denote each variable domain {0,1,2,3}. For each constraint we have allowed pairs. Let's list them clearly:\n\nc1 (a,b): allowed (a,b) pairs: (3,2), (2,3), (3,1), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1). So allowed combos:\n- a=3,b=2\n- a=2,b=3\n- a=3,b=1\n- a=0,b=2\n- a=1,b=1\n- a=0,b=1\n\nc2 (b,c): allowed (b,c):\n(2,1), (0,2), (3,2), (0,0), (3,1), (0,3)\nSo combos:\nb=2,c=1\nb=0,c=2\nb=3,c=2\nb=0,c=0\nb=3,c=1\nb=0,c=3\n\nc3 (c,d): allowed (c,d):\n(1,2), (2,2), (2,1), (3,2), (0,1), (0,0)\nSo combos:\nc=1,d=2\nc=2,d=2\nc=2,d=1\nc=3,d=2\nc=0,d=1\nc=0,d=0\n\nc4 (d,a): allowed (d,a):\n(2,3), (2,2), (3,0), (1,3), (3,3), (0,1)\nSo combos:\nd=2,a=3\nd=2,a=2\nd=3,a=0\nd=1,a=3\nd=3,a=3\nd=0,a=1\n\nc5 (e,f): allowed (e,f):\n(0,1), (1,1), (2,2), (0,2), (3,3), (2,1)\nSo combos:\ne=0,f=1\ne=1,f=1\ne=2,f=2\ne=0,f=2\ne=3,f=3\ne=2,f=1\n\nc6 (f,g): allowed (f,g):\n(1,3), (2,3), (0,1), (2,0), (1,1), (1,0)\nSo combos:\nf=1,g=3\nf=2,g=3\nf=0,g=1\nf=2,g=0\nf=1,g=1\nf=1,g=0\n\nc7 (g,h): allowed (g,h):\n(3,2), (3,1), (2,1), (1,0), (0,0), (3,0)\nSo combos:\ng=3,h=2\ng=3,h=1\ng=2,h=1\ng=1,h=0\ng=0,h=0\ng=3,h=0\n\nc8 (h,e): allowed (h,e):\n(2,0), (1,1), (2,2), (2,1), (0,3), (1,2)\nSo combos:\nh=2,e=0\nh=1,e=1\nh=2,e=2\nh=2,e=1\nh=0,e=3\nh=1,e=2\n\nc9 (i,j): allowed (i,j):\n(0,3), (0,2), (0,0), (1,1), (3,2), (2,3)\nSo combos:\ni=0,j=3\ni=0,j=2\ni=0,j=0\ni=1,j=1\ni=3,j=2\ni=2,j=3\n\nc10 (j,k): allowed (j,k):\n(3,0), (0,2), (1,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2)\nSo combos:\nj=3,k=0\nj=0,k=2\nj=1,k=0\nj=0,k=1\nj=1,k=3\nj=3,k=2\n\nc11 (k,l): allowed (k,l):\n(0,2), (1,3), (3,3), (2,0), (3,2), (1,0)\nSo combos:\nk=0,l=2\nk=1,l=3\nk=3,l=3\nk=2,l=0\nk=3,l=2\nk=1,l=0\n\nc12 (l,i): allowed (l,i):\n(2,0), (1,1), (3,3), (2,3), (2,2), (3,1)\nSo combos:\nl=2,i=0\nl=1,i=1\nl=3,i=3\nl=2,i=3\nl=2,i=2\nl=3,i=1\n\nc13 (m,n): allowed (m,n):\n(0,1), (2,2), (0,3), (3,0), (2,1), (2,0)\nSo combos:\nm=0,n=1\nm=2,n=2\nm=0,n=3\nm=3,n=0\nm=2,n=1\nm=2,n=0\n\nc14 (n,o): allowed (n,o):\n(1,3), (3,2), (3,0), (0,1), (2,0), (0,3)\nSo combos:\nn=1,o=3\nn=3,o=2\nn=3,o=0\nn=0,o=1\nn=2,o=0\nn=0,o=3\n\nc15 (o,p): allowed (o,p):\n(3,2), (0,2), (3,3), (0,1), (2,1), (1,1)\nSo combos:\no=3,p=2\no=0,p=2\no=3,p=3\no=0,p=1\no=2,p=1\no=1,p=1\n\nc16 (p,m): allowed (p,m):\n(2,0), (3,3), (0,0), (1,2), (1,3), (2,3)\nSo combos:\np=2,m=0\np=3,m=3\np=0,m=0\np=1,m=2\np=1,m=3\np=2,m=3\n\nc17 (b,e): allowed (b,e):\n(2,0), (0,2)\nSo b=2,e=0 or b=0,e=2.\n\nc18 (f,i): allowed (f,i):\n(1,0), (2,1)\nSo f=1,i=0 or f=2,i=1.\n\nc19 (j,m): allowed (j,m):\n(3,0), (0,0)\nSo j=3,m=0 or j=0,m=0.\n\nc20 (n,a): allowed (n,a):\n(1,3), (1,0)\nSo n=1,a=3 or n=1,a=0.\n\nWe need to satisfy all constraints.\n\nWe need to produce an assignment for a..p.\n\nWe can try systematic solving: treat each cycle plus bridging constraints as a larger cycle.\n\nWe have four cycles: Cycle A: a-b-c-d-a; Cycle B: e-f-g-h-e; Cycle C: i-j-k-l-i; Cycle D: m-n-o-p-m. Bridges: b-e, f-i, j-m, n-a.\n\nThus we have a \"global\" cycle linking variables: a->b->e->f->i->j->m->n->o->p->m? Wait careful: Actually the bridges go: b-e (c17) connects b to e; f-i (c18) connects f to i; j-m (c19) connects j to m; n-a (c20) connects n to a. So the global cycle is: a->b->e->f->i->j->m->n->a (closing). But also there are other constraints linking within each cycle.\n\nThus we need to find assignments for each cycle consistent with local constraints and these bridging constraints.\n\nWe can approach by enumerating possibilities for each bridging pair and then propagate constraints.\n\nLet's denote possibilities for each bridging pair:\n\nc17: (b,e) either (2,0) or (0,2).\nc18: (f,i) either (1,0) or (2,1).\nc19: (j,m) either (3,0) or (0,0).\nc20: (n,a) either (1,3) or (1,0).\n\nThus we have 2^4 = 16 combos for the bridging variables. For each combo, we need to see if we can assign the rest of variables in each cycle to satisfy local constraints.\n\nWe need to also consider that each cycle's variables must satisfy local constraints, which are independent except for the bridging constraints.\n\nThus we can treat each cycle as a CSP with given start variable values (the bridging variable) and constraints that form a cycle.\n\nCycle A: variables a,b,c,d with constraints:\nc1: (a,b)\nc2: (b,c)\nc3: (c,d)\nc4: (d,a)\n\nWe have bridging constraints: b is given by c17 (b from c17) and a is given by c20 (a from c20). So we have a and b values predetermined for each combo.\n\nThus for each combo we need to find c,d such that the constraints hold: (b,c) allowed (c2), (c,d) allowed (c3), (d,a) allowed (c4). So we need to find c,d values in domain that satisfy these three constraints given a,b.\n\nSimilarly Cycle B: variables e,f,g,h with constraints:\nc5: (e,f)\nc6: (f,g)\nc7: (g,h)\nc8: (h,e)\n\nBridging: e from c17 (b-e), f from c18 (f-i). So e is known (via c17) and f is known (via c18). Need to find g,h satisfying constraints.\n\nCycle C: variables i,j,k,l with constraints:\nc9: (i,j)\nc10: (j,k)\nc11: (k,l)\nc12: (l,i)\n\nBridging: i from c18 (f-i), j from c19 (j-m). So i and j known, need to find k,l satisfying constraints.\n\nCycle D: variables m,n,o,p with constraints:\nc13: (m,n)\nc14: (n,o)\nc15: (o,p)\nc16: (p,m)\n\nBridging: m from c19 (j-m), n from c20 (n-a). So m,n known, need to find o,p satisfying constraints.\n\nThus we need to find assignments for each cycle consistent with given start values.\n\nThus we can enumerate all combos for bridging pairs, then for each combo solve each cycle as a small CSP.\n\nWe can do manual reasoning or systematic search.\n\nWe need to find at least one assignment. Let's attempt to find one manually.\n\nFirst, list all possible values for each bridging variable:\n\nc17: (b,e) = (2,0) or (0,2).\n\nc18: (f,i) = (1,0) or (2,1).\n\nc19: (j,m) = (3,0) or (0,0).\n\nc20: (n,a) = (1,3) or (1,0).\n\nThus a can be 3 or 0; b can be 2 or 0; e can be 0 or 2; f can be 1 or 2; i can be 0 or 1; j can be 3 or 0; m can be 0 (both possibilities give m=0); n can be 1; a is 3 or 0 accordingly.\n\nSo m always 0 (since both options give m=0). So m=0 fixed. Good.\n\nNow n is 1 (both options have n=1). So n=1 fixed.\n\nThus we have n=1, m=0.\n\nNow a is either 3 or 0 (depending on c20). b is either 2 or 0 (c17). e is either 0 (if b=2) or 2 (if b=0). f is either 1 (if i=0) or 2 (if i=1). i is either 0 (if f=1) or 1 (if f=2). j is either 3 (if m=0) or 0 (if m=0). Since m=0, both options have m=0, but j can be 3 or 0. So j can be 3 or 0.\n\nThus we have many combos.\n\nNow we need to satisfy each cycle.\n\nLet's write constraints for each cycle in terms of known values.\n\nCycle A: given a and b, find c,d.\n\nc2: (b,c) must be in allowed pairs for c2. c3: (c,d) allowed. c4: (d,a) allowed.\n\nThus we need to find c,d ∈ {0,1,2,3} such that:\n\n(b,c) ∈ C2\n(c,d) ∈ C3\n(d,a) ∈ C4\n\nWhere C2 = allowed pairs for (b,c) from c2. Since b is known, we can find possible c values.\n\nSimilarly C3: allowed pairs for (c,d) from c3; C4: allowed for (d,a) from c4.\n\nThus we can for each (a,b) pair compute possible c,d.\n\nSimilarly Cycle B: given e and f, find g,h.\n\nConstraints:\nc5: (e,f) allowed (but we know e,f from bridging; they must satisfy c5 as well? Actually c5 is a constraint linking e and f. So we must also satisfy c5: (e,f) must be in allowed pairs for c5. So e,f must satisfy both c5 and bridging constraints. So we need to ensure that the bridging pair (e,f) is also allowed by c5. Similarly f,i must satisfy c18 (already bridging) and also c6? Wait c6 is (f,g) but f appears there, not i. So c5 is independent.\n\nThus we need to check that the bridging pair (b,e) and (e,f) must both be allowed: c5 for (e,f) and c17 for (b,e). So we need to ensure that the chosen values for b,e also satisfy c5? No, c5 is (e,f). So we need to check (e,f) in c5.\n\nSimilarly bridging (f,i) must also satisfy c6? No, c6 is (f,g). So bridging (f,i) does not need to satisfy any other constraints besides c18. However, there is also c5 for (e,f). So we need to ensure (e,f) is allowed by c5.\n\nThus for each bridging combo, we must check that (e,f) is allowed by c5.\n\nSimilarly bridging (i,j) is not a direct constraint; i and j are separate cycles: i is part of cycle C, j part of cycle C as well, but there is c9 linking i,j. So (i,j) must satisfy c9. So bridging (f,i) ensures i, but j is separate. So (i,j) must be allowed by c9.\n\nSimilarly bridging (j,m) is c19; also c10 links (j,k). So j must satisfy both c9 (i,j) and c10 (j,k) and c19 (j,m). So j must be consistent with i (c9) and m (c19). So we must ensure (i,j) and (j,m) both allowed.\n\nSimilarly bridging (n,a) is c20; also c13 links (m,n) and c14 (n,o). So n must satisfy both c13 (m,n) and c14 (n,o) and c20 (n,a). So need to ensure (m,n) allowed (c13), (n,o) allowed (c14), and (n,a) allowed (c20). So n must be consistent with m and a and o.\n\nThus overall we need to satisfy all constraints simultaneously.\n\nThus we need to find assignments for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p that satisfy all constraints.\n\nWe can solve using systematic search manually. Let's attempt to enumerate possibilities for bridging combos and then solve each cycle.\n\nWe can reduce search space by focusing on constraints.\n\nFirst, note that m=0, n=1. So c13 (m,n) must be allowed: allowed pairs for (m,n) are (0,1), (2,2), (0,3), (3,0), (2,1), (2,0). So (m,n) = (0,1) is allowed (first entry). So c13 satisfied automatically for m=0,n=1. Good.\n\nNow c20: (n,a) allowed pairs are (1,3) and (1,0). Since n=1, a must be 3 or 0. So a ∈ {3,0}.\n\nThus a is either 3 or 0.\n\nNow c4: (d,a) must be allowed. So depending on a, we can restrict d.\n\nc4 allowed pairs: (2,3), (2,2), (3,0), (1,3), (3,3), (0,1). So for a=3, allowed d values: from pairs where second component is a=3: (2,3) => d=2; (1,3) => d=1; (3,3) => d=3. So d ∈ {2,1,3} for a=3. For a=0, allowed pairs where second component is a=0: (3,0) => d=3; (0,1) is a=1; (0,? Actually (0,1) is a=1; (0,? ) Wait c4 allowed pairs list: (2,3), (2,2), (3,0), (1,3), (3,3), (0,1). So a=0 only appears in (3,0). So for a=0, d must be 3. So d=3.\n\nThus if a=0, d is forced to 3. If a=3, d ∈ {1,2,3}.\n\nNow c1: (a,b) allowed pairs: list: (3,2), (2,3), (3,1), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1). So for each a, possible b values:\n\nIf a=3: allowed b values: from pairs where a=3: (3,2) => b=2; (3,1) => b=1; also (2,3) is a=2 not relevant; (0,2) a=0; (1,1) a=1; (0,1) a=0. So for a=3, b can be 2 or 1.\n\nIf a=0: allowed b values: from (0,2) => b=2; (0,1) => b=1. So for a=0, b ∈ {2,1}.\n\nThus b can be 2 or 1 depending on a.\n\nNow c17: (b,e) allowed pairs: (2,0) or (0,2). So b and e must be one of those two combos: either (b=2,e=0) or (b=0,e=2). So b must be either 2 or 0, and e is determined accordingly: if b=2 then e=0; if b=0 then e=2.\n\nThus b must be 2 or 0. But from above, b can be 2 or 1 (if a=3) or 2 or 1 (if a=0). So b=1 is not allowed by c17. So b cannot be 1. Therefore b must be 2. Then from c17, e=0.\n\nThus we deduce b=2, e=0.\n\nThus b=2, e=0.\n\nNow b=2, from c1 we have a must be such that (a,b) allowed: allowed pairs with b=2: from c1 list: (3,2) => a=3; (0,2) => a=0. So a can be 3 or 0. Both are allowed.\n\nThus a ∈ {3,0} as earlier.\n\nThus far we have b=2, e=0, m=0, n=1.\n\nNow c2: (b,c) allowed pairs: b=2, so we need c such that (2,c) is allowed in c2. c2 allowed pairs: (2,1), (0,2), (3,2), (0,0), (3,1), (0,3). So (2,c) appears only in (2,1) => c=1. So c must be 1. So c=1 forced.\n\nThus c=1.\n\nNow c3: (c,d) allowed pairs: c=1, so need (1,d) allowed in c3. c3 allowed pairs: (1,2), (2,2), (2,1), (3,2), (0,1), (0,0). So (1,d) appears only in (1,2) => d=2. So d=2.\n\nThus d=2.\n\nNow c4: (d,a) must be allowed: we have d=2, a ∈ {3,0}. Check allowed pairs for (d,a) in c4: allowed pairs: (2,3), (2,2), (3,0), (1,3), (3,3), (0,1). So (2,3) is allowed: d=2,a=3 works. (2,2) is allowed: d=2,a=2 but a cannot be 2. So only (2,3) works. So a must be 3. So a=3.\n\nThus a=3.\n\nThus a=3, b=2, c=1, d=2.\n\nNow check c1: (a,b) = (3,2) which is allowed (yes). c2: (2,1) allowed (yes). c3: (1,2) allowed (yes). c4: (2,3) allowed (yes). So Cycle A solved uniquely: a=3,b=2,c=1,d=2.\n\nThus a=3, b=2.\n\nNow we have b=2, e=0 from c17.\n\nNow we need to satisfy Cycle B: variables e,f,g,h with constraints c5,c6,c7,c8. Known e=0, unknown f,g,h.\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed: e=0, so (0,f) must be in allowed pairs for c5. c5 allowed pairs: (0,1), (1,1), (2,2), (0,2), (3,3), (2,1). So (0,f) allowed when f=1 (0,1) or f=2 (0,2). So f ∈ {1,2}.\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed: depends on f.\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed.\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed: e=0, so (h,0) must be in allowed pairs for c8. c8 allowed pairs: (2,0), (1,1), (2,2), (2,1), (0,3), (1,2). So (h,0) appears only in (2,0) => h=2. So h=2 forced.\n\nThus h=2.\n\nNow c8: (h,e) = (2,0) is allowed (yes). Good.\n\nNow we have h=2.\n\nNow c7: (g,h) = (g,2) must be allowed. c7 allowed pairs: (3,2), (3,1), (2,1), (1,0), (0,0), (3,0). So (g,2) appears only in (3,2) => g=3. So g=3 forced.\n\nThus g=3.\n\nNow c6: (f,g) = (f,3) must be allowed. c6 allowed pairs: (1,3), (2,3), (0,1), (2,0), (1,1), (1,0). So (f,3) appears in (1,3) => f=1, and (2,3) => f=2. So f ∈ {1,2}.\n\nThus f can be 1 or 2, consistent with earlier from c5 (f ∈ {1,2}). So both possible.\n\nThus Cycle B yields g=3, h=2, f∈{1,2}, e=0.\n\nNow we need to satisfy bridging constraints c18: (f,i) allowed: (f,i) must be either (1,0) or (2,1). So if f=1, then i must be 0. If f=2, then i must be 1.\n\nThus i is determined: i = 0 if f=1; i = 1 if f=2.\n\nThus i ∈ {0,1} accordingly.\n\nNow we need to satisfy Cycle C: variables i,j,k,l with constraints c9,c10,c11,c12. Known i (depending on f), j from c19, unknown k,l.\n\nc9: (i,j) allowed: need to check allowed pairs for (i,j). c9 allowed pairs: (0,3), (0,2), (0,0), (1,1), (3,2), (2,3). So depending on i and j.\n\nc10: (j,k) allowed: c10 allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,2), (1,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2).\n\nc11: (k,l) allowed: c11 allowed pairs: (0,2), (1,3), (3,3), (2,0), (3,2), (1,0).\n\nc12: (l,i) allowed: c12 allowed pairs: (2,0), (1,1), (3,3), (2,3), (2,2), (3,1).\n\nNow bridging c19: (j,m) allowed: (j,m) must be either (3,0) or (0,0). Since m=0, j must be 3 or 0. So j ∈ {3,0}.\n\nThus we have two possibilities for j: 3 or 0.\n\nNow we need to satisfy c9: (i,j) must be allowed. So given i (0 or 1) and j (3 or 0), we need to see which combos are allowed.\n\nList allowed (i,j) pairs from c9:\n\n- (0,3) allowed.\n- (0,2) allowed.\n- (0,0) allowed.\n- (1,1) allowed.\n- (3,2) allowed.\n- (2,3) allowed.\n\nThus allowed combos: (i,j) can be (0,3), (0,2), (0,0), (1,1), (3,2), (2,3). So for i=0, j can be 3,2,0. For i=1, j must be 1 (but j is only 3 or 0). So i=1 is not possible because j cannot be 1. Thus i cannot be 1. Therefore i must be 0. Then j must be one of {3,2,0} allowed. But j is limited to {3,0} from c19. So j can be 3 or 0. Both are allowed with i=0: (0,3) allowed, (0,0) allowed. So both possible.\n\nThus i=0, j ∈ {3,0}.\n\nThus i=0 => f must be 1 (since i=0 only when f=1). So f=1. Then g=3, h=2, e=0 as before. So we have f=1, i=0.\n\nThus we have determined f=1, i=0.\n\nNow we need to find k,l consistent with j.\n\nNow we have j ∈ {3,0}. Let's explore both possibilities.\n\nCase 1: j=3.\n\nThen c9: (i,j) = (0,3) allowed (yes). c19: (j,m) = (3,0) allowed (yes). So j=3 works.\n\nNow we need to find k,l such that:\n\nc10: (j,k) = (3,k) must be allowed. c10 allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,2), (1,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2). So (3,k) allowed when k=0 (pair (3,0)) or k=2 (pair (3,2)). So k ∈ {0,2}.\n\nc11: (k,l) must be allowed. c11 allowed pairs: (0,2), (1,3), (3,3), (2,0), (3,2), (1,0). So for each possible k, we need l.\n\nc12: (l,i) = (l,0) must be allowed. c12 allowed pairs: (2,0), (1,1), (3,3), (2,3), (2,2), (3,1). So (l,0) appears only in (2,0) => l=2. So l must be 2.\n\nThus l=2.\n\nNow check c12: (l,i) = (2,0) allowed (yes). Good.\n\nNow we need to ensure c11: (k,l) = (k,2) allowed. c11 allowed pairs include (0,2) => k=0 works; (3,2) => k=3 works; also (??) (??) Actually (3,2) is allowed. Also (??) (??) (??) (??) (??). Let's list all c11 pairs: (0,2), (1,3), (3,3), (2,0), (3,2), (1,0). So (k,2) allowed for k=0 (0,2) and k=3 (3,2). So k can be 0 or 3. But earlier from c10, k ∈ {0,2}. Intersection gives k=0. So k must be 0.\n\nThus k=0, l=2.\n\nNow check c11: (0,2) allowed (yes). c10: (3,0) allowed (yes). So case j=3 yields a consistent assignment: i=0, j=3, k=0, l=2.\n\nNow we need to satisfy Cycle D: variables m,n,o,p with constraints c13,c14,c15,c16. Known m=0, n=1. Need to find o,p.\n\nc13: (m,n) = (0,1) allowed (yes). Already satisfied.\n\nc14: (n,o) = (1,o) must be allowed. c14 allowed pairs: (1,3), (3,2), (3,0), (0,1), (2,0), (0,3). So (1,o) appears only in (1,3) => o=3. So o=3 forced.\n\nThus o=3.\n\nNow c15: (o,p) = (3,p) must be allowed. c15 allowed pairs: (3,2), (0,2), (3,3), (0,1), (2,1), (1,1). So (3,p) allowed when p=2 (3,2) or p=3 (3,3). So p ∈ {2,3}.\n\nc16: (p,m) = (p,0) must be allowed. c16 allowed pairs: (2,0), (3,3), (0,0), (1,2), (1,3), (2,3). So (p,0) appears only in (2,0) => p=2. Also (0,0) is (p=0) but p is 2 or 3 from c15. So p must be 2 to satisfy c16. So p=2.\n\nThus p=2.\n\nNow check c15: (3,2) allowed (yes). So Cycle D solved: m=0,n=1,o=3,p=2.\n\nNow we need to verify bridging c20: (n,a) = (1,3) must be allowed. c20 allowed pairs: (1,3) and (1,0). So (1,3) is allowed. Good.\n\nThus all constraints satisfied for case j=3.\n\nNow we need to verify that we haven't missed any constraints linking other variables: The only bridging constraints are c17 (b,e) which we satisfied (b=2,e=0). c18 (f,i) = (1,0) allowed (yes). c19 (j,m) = (3,0) allowed (yes). c20 (n,a) = (1,3) allowed (yes). All local constraints satisfied.\n\nThus we have a consistent assignment:\n\na=3\nb=2\nc=1\nd=2\ne=0\nf=1\ng=3\nh=2\ni=0\nj=3\nk=0\nl=2\nm=0\nn=1\no=3\np=2\n\nLet's double-check each constraint to ensure no mistake.\n\nList constraints with assigned values:\n\nc1 (a,b): (3,2) allowed? Yes (3,2) is in allowed pairs.\n\nc2 (b,c): (2,1) allowed? Yes (2,1) is in allowed pairs.\n\nc3 (c,d): (1,2) allowed? Yes (1,2) is allowed.\n\nc4 (d,a): (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) is allowed.\n\nc5 (e,f): (0,1) allowed? Yes (0,1) is allowed.\n\nc6 (f,g): (1,3) allowed? Yes (1,3) is allowed.\n\nc7 (g,h): (3,2) allowed? Yes (3,2) is allowed.\n\nc8 (h,e): (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is allowed.\n\nc9 (i,j): (0,3) allowed? Yes (0,3) is allowed.\n\nc10 (j,k): (3,0) allowed? Yes (3,0) is allowed.\n\nc11 (k,l): (0,2) allowed? Yes (0,2) is allowed.\n\nc12 (l,i): (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is allowed.\n\nc13 (m,n): (0,1) allowed? Yes (0,1) is allowed.\n\nc14 (n,o): (1,3) allowed? Yes (1,3) is allowed.\n\nc15 (o,p): (3,2) allowed? Yes (3,2) is allowed.\n\nc16 (p,m): (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is allowed.\n\nc17 (b,e): (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is allowed.\n\nc18 (f,i): (1,0) allowed? Yes (1,0) is allowed.\n\nc19 (j,m): (3,0) allowed? Yes (3,0) is allowed.\n\nc20 (n,a): (1,3) allowed? Yes (1,3) is allowed.\n\nAll constraints satisfied.\n\nThus we have found a valid assignment.\n\nNow we need to output exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON; raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\n\nThe template is:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"CONCLUDE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6582-raw-integrator-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"conclusion\": {\n      \"assignment\": {\n        \"a\": 0,\n        \"b\": 0,\n        \"c\": 0,\n        \"d\": 0,\n        \"e\": 0,\n        \"f\": 0,\n        \"g\": 0,\n        \"h\": 0,\n        \"i\": 0,\n        \"j\": 0,\n        \"k\": 0,\n        \"l\": 0,\n        \"m\": 0,\n        \"n\": 0,\n        \"o\": 0,\n        \"p\": 0\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6582\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6582\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"raw-integrator\"\n}\n\nWe need to edit only the sixteen integer values in conclusion.assignment. So we replace the zeros with our assignment values.\n\nThus final JSON will have assignment with values:\n\n\"a\": 3,\n\"b\": 2,\n\"c\": 1,\n\"d\": 2,\n\"e\": 0,\n\"f\": 1,\n\"g\": 3,\n\"h\": 2,\n\"i\": 0,\n\"j\": 3,\n\"k\": 0,\n\"l\": 2,\n\"m\": 0,\n\"n\": 1,\n\"o\": 3,\n\"p\": 2\n\nWe must ensure JSON format: numbers are integers, no extra spaces? But spaces are okay. The output must be raw JSON, no markdown fences.\n\nThus final answer:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"CONCLUDE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6582-raw-integrator-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"conclusion\": {\n      \"assignment\": {\n        \"a\": 3,\n        \"b\": 2,\n        \"c\": 1,\n        \"d\": 2,\n        \"e\": 0,\n        \"f\": 1,\n        \"g\": 3,\n        \"h\": 2,\n        \"i\": 0,\n        \"j\": 3,\n        \"k\": 0,\n        \"l\": 2,\n        \"m\": 0,\n        \"n\": 1,\n        \"o\": 3,\n        \"p\": 2\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6582\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6582\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"raw-integrator\"\n}\n\nMake sure no trailing commas. All good.\n\nThus output this JSON.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need to solve the CSP. Let's restate:\n\nWe have variables a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p each domain {0,1,2,3}. There are constraints c1..c16 for pairs of variables, and also c17..c20 bridging constraints between components: c17 is between b and e, allowed pairs: [2,0] and [0,2]; c18 between f and i allowed [1,0] and [2,1]; c19 between j and m allowed [3,0] and [0,0]; c20 between n and a allowed [1,3] and [1,0].\n\nWe need to find a complete assignment satisfying all constraints.\n\nConstraints c1..c16 are local constraints for adjacent variables in a cycle of components: c1: a,b; c2: b,c; c3: c,d; c4: d,a; c5: e,f; c6: f,g; c7: g,h; c8: h,e; c9: i,j; c10: j,k; c11: k,l; c12: l,i; c13: m,n; c14: n,o; c15: o,p; c16: p,m. So there are two independent cycles: (a,b,c,d) and (e,f,g,h) and (i,j,k,l) and (m,n,o,p). Actually each of those four cycles is separate: c1-c4 is a cycle of a,b,c,d; c5-c8 is cycle of e,f,g,h; c9-c12 is cycle of i,j,k,l; c13-c16 is cycle of m,n,o,p. So we need to assign each cycle satisfying its local constraints and also bridging constraints across cycles: c17 connects b (from cycle1) to e (cycle2). c18 connects f (cycle2) to i (cycle3). c19 connects j (cycle3) to m (cycle4). c20 connects n (cycle4) to a (cycle1). So overall it's a larger cycle linking all four cycles.\n\nWe need to find any assignment (maybe unique) that satisfies all constraints.\n\nApproach: brute force search manually? There are 4^16 = 4,294,967,296 possibilities, too many. But we can reason.\n\nLet's denote each variable domain {0,1,2,3}. For each constraint we have allowed pairs. Let's list them clearly:\n\nc1 (a,b): allowed (a,b) pairs: (3,2), (2,3), (3,1), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1). So allowed combos:\n- a=3,b=2\n- a=2,b=3\n- a=3,b=1\n- a=0,b=2\n- a=1,b=1\n- a=0,b=1\n\nc2 (b,c): allowed (b,c):\n(2,1), (0,2), (3,2), (0,0), (3,1), (0,3)\nSo combos:\nb=2,c=1\nb=0,c=2\nb=3,c=2\nb=0,c=0\nb=3,c=1\nb=0,c=3\n\nc3 (c,d): allowed (c,d):\n(1,2), (2,2), (2,1), (3,2), (0,1), (0,0)\nSo combos:\nc=1,d=2\nc=2,d=2\nc=2,d=1\nc=3,d=2\nc=0,d=1\nc=0,d=0\n\nc4 (d,a): allowed (d,a):\n(2,3), (2,2), (3,0), (1,3), (3,3), (0,1)\nSo combos:\nd=2,a=3\nd=2,a=2\nd=3,a=0\nd=1,a=3\nd=3,a=3\nd=0,a=1\n\nc5 (e,f): allowed (e,f):\n(0,1), (1,1), (2,2), (0,2), (3,3), (2,1)\nSo combos:\ne=0,f=1\ne=1,f=1\ne=2,f=2\ne=0,f=2\ne=3,f=3\ne=2,f=1\n\nc6 (f,g): allowed (f,g):\n(1,3), (2,3), (0,1), (2,0), (1,1), (1,0)\nSo combos:\nf=1,g=3\nf=2,g=3\nf=0,g=1\nf=2,g=0\nf=1,g=1\nf=1,g=0\n\nc7 (g,h): allowed (g,h):\n(3,2), (3,1), (2,1), (1,0), (0,0), (3,0)\nSo combos:\ng=3,h=2\ng=3,h=1\ng=2,h=1\ng=1,h=0\ng=0,h=0\ng=3,h=0\n\nc8 (h,e): allowed (h,e):\n(2,0), (1,1), (2,2), (2,1), (0,3), (1,2)\nSo combos:\nh=2,e=0\nh=1,e=1\nh=2,e=2\nh=2,e=1\nh=0,e=3\nh=1,e=2\n\nc9 (i,j): allowed (i,j):\n(0,3), (0,2), (0,0), (1,1), (3,2), (2,3)\nSo combos:\ni=0,j=3\ni=0,j=2\ni=0,j=0\ni=1,j=1\ni=3,j=2\ni=2,j=3\n\nc10 (j,k): allowed (j,k):\n(3,0), (0,2), (1,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2)\nSo combos:\nj=3,k=0\nj=0,k=2\nj=1,k=0\nj=0,k=1\nj=1,k=3\nj=3,k=2\n\nc11 (k,l): allowed (k,l):\n(0,2), (1,3), (3,3), (2,0), (3,2), (1,0)\nSo combos:\nk=0,l=2\nk=1,l=3\nk=3,l=3\nk=2,l=0\nk=3,l=2\nk=1,l=0\n\nc12 (l,i): allowed (l,i):\n(2,0), (1,1), (3,3), (2,3), (2,2), (3,1)\nSo combos:\nl=2,i=0\nl=1,i=1\nl=3,i=3\nl=2,i=3\nl=2,i=2\nl=3,i=1\n\nc13 (m,n): allowed (m,n):\n(0,1), (2,2), (0,3), (3,0), (2,1), (2,0)\nSo combos:\nm=0,n=1\nm=2,n=2\nm=0,n=3\nm=3,n=0\nm=2,n=1\nm=2,n=0\n\nc14 (n,o): allowed (n,o):\n(1,3), (3,2), (3,0), (0,1), (2,0), (0,3)\nSo combos:\nn=1,o=3\nn=3,o=2\nn=3,o=0\nn=0,o=1\nn=2,o=0\nn=0,o=3\n\nc15 (o,p): allowed (o,p):\n(3,2), (0,2), (3,3), (0,1), (2,1), (1,1)\nSo combos:\no=3,p=2\no=0,p=2\no=3,p=3\no=0,p=1\no=2,p=1\no=1,p=1\n\nc16 (p,m): allowed (p,m):\n(2,0), (3,3), (0,0), (1,2), (1,3), (2,3)\nSo combos:\np=2,m=0\np=3,m=3\np=0,m=0\np=1,m=2\np=1,m=3\np=2,m=3\n\nc17 (b,e): allowed (b,e):\n(2,0), (0,2)\nSo b=2,e=0 or b=0,e=2.\n\nc18 (f,i): allowed (f,i):\n(1,0), (2,1)\nSo f=1,i=0 or f=2,i=1.\n\nc19 (j,m): allowed (j,m):\n(3,0), (0,0)\nSo j=3,m=0 or j=0,m=0.\n\nc20 (n,a): allowed (n,a):\n(1,3), (1,0)\nSo n=1,a=3 or n=1,a=0.\n\nWe need to satisfy all constraints.\n\nWe need to produce an assignment for a..p.\n\nWe can try systematic solving: treat each cycle plus bridging constraints as a larger cycle.\n\nWe have four cycles: Cycle A: a-b-c-d-a; Cycle B: e-f-g-h-e; Cycle C: i-j-k-l-i; Cycle D: m-n-o-p-m. Bridges: b-e, f-i, j-m, n-a.\n\nThus we have a \"global\" cycle linking variables: a->b->e->f->i->j->m->n->o->p->m? Wait careful: Actually the bridges go: b-e (c17) connects b to e; f-i (c18) connects f to i; j-m (c19) connects j to m; n-a (c20) connects n to a. So the global cycle is: a->b->e->f->i->j->m->n->a (closing). But also there are other constraints linking within each cycle.\n\nThus we need to find assignments for each cycle consistent with local constraints and these bridging constraints.\n\nWe can approach by enumerating possibilities for each bridging pair and then propagate constraints.\n\nLet's denote possibilities for each bridging pair:\n\nc17: (b,e) either (2,0) or (0,2).\nc18: (f,i) either (1,0) or (2,1).\nc19: (j,m) either (3,0) or (0,0).\nc20: (n,a) either (1,3) or (1,0).\n\nThus we have 2^4 = 16 combos for the bridging variables. For each combo, we need to see if we can assign the rest of variables in each cycle to satisfy local constraints.\n\nWe need to also consider that each cycle's variables must satisfy local constraints, which are independent except for the bridging constraints.\n\nThus we can treat each cycle as a CSP with given start variable values (the bridging variable) and constraints that form a cycle.\n\nCycle A: variables a,b,c,d with constraints:\nc1: (a,b)\nc2: (b,c)\nc3: (c,d)\nc4: (d,a)\n\nWe have bridging constraints: b is given by c17 (b from c17) and a is given by c20 (a from c20). So we have a and b values predetermined for each combo.\n\nThus for each combo we need to find c,d such that the constraints hold: (b,c) allowed (c2), (c,d) allowed (c3), (d,a) allowed (c4). So we need to find c,d values in domain that satisfy these three constraints given a,b.\n\nSimilarly Cycle B: variables e,f,g,h with constraints:\nc5: (e,f)\nc6: (f,g)\nc7: (g,h)\nc8: (h,e)\n\nBridging: e from c17 (b-e), f from c18 (f-i). So e is known (via c17) and f is known (via c18). Need to find g,h satisfying constraints.\n\nCycle C: variables i,j,k,l with constraints:\nc9: (i,j)\nc10: (j,k)\nc11: (k,l)\nc12: (l,i)\n\nBridging: i from c18 (f-i), j from c19 (j-m). So i and j known, need to find k,l satisfying constraints.\n\nCycle D: variables m,n,o,p with constraints:\nc13: (m,n)\nc14: (n,o)\nc15: (o,p)\nc16: (p,m)\n\nBridging: m from c19 (j-m), n from c20 (n-a). So m,n known, need to find o,p satisfying constraints.\n\nThus we need to find assignments for each cycle consistent with given start values.\n\nThus we can enumerate all combos for bridging pairs, then for each combo solve each cycle as a small CSP.\n\nWe can do manual reasoning or systematic search.\n\nWe need to find at least one assignment. Let's attempt to find one manually.\n\nFirst, list all possible values for each bridging variable:\n\nc17: (b,e) = (2,0) or (0,2).\n\nc18: (f,i) = (1,0) or (2,1).\n\nc19: (j,m) = (3,0) or (0,0).\n\nc20: (n,a) = (1,3) or (1,0).\n\nThus a can be 3 or 0; b can be 2 or 0; e can be 0 or 2; f can be 1 or 2; i can be 0 or 1; j can be 3 or 0; m can be 0 (both possibilities give m=0); n can be 1; a is 3 or 0 accordingly.\n\nSo m always 0 (since both options give m=0). So m=0 fixed. Good.\n\nNow n is 1 (both options have n=1). So n=1 fixed.\n\nThus we have n=1, m=0.\n\nNow a is either 3 or 0 (depending on c20). b is either 2 or 0 (c17). e is either 0 (if b=2) or 2 (if b=0). f is either 1 (if i=0) or 2 (if i=1). i is either 0 (if f=1) or 1 (if f=2). j is either 3 (if m=0) or 0 (if m=0). Since m=0, both options have m=0, but j can be 3 or 0. So j can be 3 or 0.\n\nThus we have many combos.\n\nNow we need to satisfy each cycle.\n\nLet's write constraints for each cycle in terms of known values.\n\nCycle A: given a and b, find c,d.\n\nc2: (b,c) must be in allowed pairs for c2. c3: (c,d) allowed. c4: (d,a) allowed.\n\nThus we need to find c,d ∈ {0,1,2,3} such that:\n\n(b,c) ∈ C2\n(c,d) ∈ C3\n(d,a) ∈ C4\n\nWhere C2 = allowed pairs for (b,c) from c2. Since b is known, we can find possible c values.\n\nSimilarly C3: allowed pairs for (c,d) from c3; C4: allowed for (d,a) from c4.\n\nThus we can for each (a,b) pair compute possible c,d.\n\nSimilarly Cycle B: given e and f, find g,h.\n\nConstraints:\nc5: (e,f) allowed (but we know e,f from bridging; they must satisfy c5 as well? Actually c5 is a constraint linking e and f. So we must also satisfy c5: (e,f) must be in allowed pairs for c5. So e,f must satisfy both c5 and bridging constraints. So we need to ensure that the bridging pair (e,f) is also allowed by c5. Similarly f,i must satisfy c18 (already bridging) and also c6? Wait c6 is (f,g) but f appears there, not i. So c5 is independent.\n\nThus we need to check that the bridging pair (b,e) and (e,f) must both be allowed: c5 for (e,f) and c17 for (b,e). So we need to ensure that the chosen values for b,e also satisfy c5? No, c5 is (e,f). So we need to check (e,f) in c5.\n\nSimilarly bridging (f,i) must also satisfy c6? No, c6 is (f,g). So bridging (f,i) does not need to satisfy any other constraints besides c18. However, there is also c5 for (e,f). So we need to ensure (e,f) is allowed by c5.\n\nThus for each bridging combo, we must check that (e,f) is allowed by c5.\n\nSimilarly bridging (i,j) is not a direct constraint; i and j are separate cycles: i is part of cycle C, j part of cycle C as well, but there is c9 linking i,j. So (i,j) must satisfy c9. So bridging (f,i) ensures i, but j is separate. So (i,j) must be allowed by c9.\n\nSimilarly bridging (j,m) is c19; also c10 links (j,k). So j must satisfy both c9 (i,j) and c10 (j,k) and c19 (j,m). So j must be consistent with i (c9) and m (c19). So we must ensure (i,j) and (j,m) both allowed.\n\nSimilarly bridging (n,a) is c20; also c13 links (m,n) and c14 (n,o). So n must satisfy both c13 (m,n) and c14 (n,o) and c20 (n,a). So need to ensure (m,n) allowed (c13), (n,o) allowed (c14), and (n,a) allowed (c20). So n must be consistent with m and a and o.\n\nThus overall we need to satisfy all constraints simultaneously.\n\nThus we need to find assignments for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p that satisfy all constraints.\n\nWe can solve using systematic search manually. Let's attempt to enumerate possibilities for bridging combos and then solve each cycle.\n\nWe can reduce search space by focusing on constraints.\n\nFirst, note that m=0, n=1. So c13 (m,n) must be allowed: allowed pairs for (m,n) are (0,1), (2,2), (0,3), (3,0), (2,1), (2,0). So (m,n) = (0,1) is allowed (first entry). So c13 satisfied automatically for m=0,n=1. Good.\n\nNow c20: (n,a) allowed pairs are (1,3) and (1,0). Since n=1, a must be 3 or 0. So a ∈ {3,0}.\n\nThus a is either 3 or 0.\n\nNow c4: (d,a) must be allowed. So depending on a, we can restrict d.\n\nc4 allowed pairs: (2,3), (2,2), (3,0), (1,3), (3,3), (0,1). So for a=3, allowed d values: from pairs where second component is a=3: (2,3) => d=2; (1,3) => d=1; (3,3) => d=3. So d ∈ {2,1,3} for a=3. For a=0, allowed pairs where second component is a=0: (3,0) => d=3; (0,1) is a=1; (0,? Actually (0,1) is a=1; (0,? ) Wait c4 allowed pairs list: (2,3), (2,2), (3,0), (1,3), (3,3), (0,1). So a=0 only appears in (3,0). So for a=0, d must be 3. So d=3.\n\nThus if a=0, d is forced to 3. If a=3, d ∈ {1,2,3}.\n\nNow c1: (a,b) allowed pairs: list: (3,2), (2,3), (3,1), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1). So for each a, possible b values:\n\nIf a=3: allowed b values: from pairs where a=3: (3,2) => b=2; (3,1) => b=1; also (2,3) is a=2 not relevant; (0,2) a=0; (1,1) a=1; (0,1) a=0. So for a=3, b can be 2 or 1.\n\nIf a=0: allowed b values: from (0,2) => b=2; (0,1) => b=1. So for a=0, b ∈ {2,1}.\n\nThus b can be 2 or 1 depending on a.\n\nNow c17: (b,e) allowed pairs: (2,0) or (0,2). So b and e must be one of those two combos: either (b=2,e=0) or (b=0,e=2). So b must be either 2 or 0, and e is determined accordingly: if b=2 then e=0; if b=0 then e=2.\n\nThus b must be 2 or 0. But from above, b can be 2 or 1 (if a=3) or 2 or 1 (if a=0). So b=1 is not allowed by c17. So b cannot be 1. Therefore b must be 2. Then from c17, e=0.\n\nThus we deduce b=2, e=0.\n\nThus b=2, e=0.\n\nNow b=2, from c1 we have a must be such that (a,b) allowed: allowed pairs with b=2: from c1 list: (3,2) => a=3; (0,2) => a=0. So a can be 3 or 0. Both are allowed.\n\nThus a ∈ {3,0} as earlier.\n\nThus far we have b=2, e=0, m=0, n=1.\n\nNow c2: (b,c) allowed pairs: b=2, so we need c such that (2,c) is allowed in c2. c2 allowed pairs: (2,1), (0,2), (3,2), (0,0), (3,1), (0,3). So (2,c) appears only in (2,1) => c=1. So c must be 1. So c=1 forced.\n\nThus c=1.\n\nNow c3: (c,d) allowed pairs: c=1, so need (1,d) allowed in c3. c3 allowed pairs: (1,2), (2,2), (2,1), (3,2), (0,1), (0,0). So (1,d) appears only in (1,2) => d=2. So d=2.\n\nThus d=2.\n\nNow c4: (d,a) must be allowed: we have d=2, a ∈ {3,0}. Check allowed pairs for (d,a) in c4: allowed pairs: (2,3), (2,2), (3,0), (1,3), (3,3), (0,1). So (2,3) is allowed: d=2,a=3 works. (2,2) is allowed: d=2,a=2 but a cannot be 2. So only (2,3) works. So a must be 3. So a=3.\n\nThus a=3.\n\nThus a=3, b=2, c=1, d=2.\n\nNow check c1: (a,b) = (3,2) which is allowed (yes). c2: (2,1) allowed (yes). c3: (1,2) allowed (yes). c4: (2,3) allowed (yes). So Cycle A solved uniquely: a=3,b=2,c=1,d=2.\n\nThus a=3, b=2.\n\nNow we have b=2, e=0 from c17.\n\nNow we need to satisfy Cycle B: variables e,f,g,h with constraints c5,c6,c7,c8. Known e=0, unknown f,g,h.\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed: e=0, so (0,f) must be in allowed pairs for c5. c5 allowed pairs: (0,1), (1,1), (2,2), (0,2), (3,3), (2,1). So (0,f) allowed when f=1 (0,1) or f=2 (0,2). So f ∈ {1,2}.\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed: depends on f.\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed.\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed: e=0, so (h,0) must be in allowed pairs for c8. c8 allowed pairs: (2,0), (1,1), (2,2), (2,1), (0,3), (1,2). So (h,0) appears only in (2,0) => h=2. So h=2 forced.\n\nThus h=2.\n\nNow c8: (h,e) = (2,0) is allowed (yes). Good.\n\nNow we have h=2.\n\nNow c7: (g,h) = (g,2) must be allowed. c7 allowed pairs: (3,2), (3,1), (2,1), (1,0), (0,0), (3,0). So (g,2) appears only in (3,2) => g=3. So g=3 forced.\n\nThus g=3.\n\nNow c6: (f,g) = (f,3) must be allowed. c6 allowed pairs: (1,3), (2,3), (0,1), (2,0), (1,1), (1,0). So (f,3) appears in (1,3) => f=1, and (2,3) => f=2. So f ∈ {1,2}.\n\nThus f can be 1 or 2, consistent with earlier from c5 (f ∈ {1,2}). So both possible.\n\nThus Cycle B yields g=3, h=2, f∈{1,2}, e=0.\n\nNow we need to satisfy bridging constraints c18: (f,i) allowed: (f,i) must be either (1,0) or (2,1). So if f=1, then i must be 0. If f=2, then i must be 1.\n\nThus i is determined: i = 0 if f=1; i = 1 if f=2.\n\nThus i ∈ {0,1} accordingly.\n\nNow we need to satisfy Cycle C: variables i,j,k,l with constraints c9,c10,c11,c12. Known i (depending on f), j from c19, unknown k,l.\n\nc9: (i,j) allowed: need to check allowed pairs for (i,j). c9 allowed pairs: (0,3), (0,2), (0,0), (1,1), (3,2), (2,3). So depending on i and j.\n\nc10: (j,k) allowed: c10 allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,2), (1,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2).\n\nc11: (k,l) allowed: c11 allowed pairs: (0,2), (1,3), (3,3), (2,0), (3,2), (1,0).\n\nc12: (l,i) allowed: c12 allowed pairs: (2,0), (1,1), (3,3), (2,3), (2,2), (3,1).\n\nNow bridging c19: (j,m) allowed: (j,m) must be either (3,0) or (0,0). Since m=0, j must be 3 or 0. So j ∈ {3,0}.\n\nThus we have two possibilities for j: 3 or 0.\n\nNow we need to satisfy c9: (i,j) must be allowed. So given i (0 or 1) and j (3 or 0), we need to see which combos are allowed.\n\nList allowed (i,j) pairs from c9:\n\n- (0,3) allowed.\n- (0,2) allowed.\n- (0,0) allowed.\n- (1,1) allowed.\n- (3,2) allowed.\n- (2,3) allowed.\n\nThus allowed combos: (i,j) can be (0,3), (0,2), (0,0), (1,1), (3,2), (2,3). So for i=0, j can be 3,2,0. For i=1, j must be 1 (but j is only 3 or 0). So i=1 is not possible because j cannot be 1. Thus i cannot be 1. Therefore i must be 0. Then j must be one of {3,2,0} allowed. But j is limited to {3,0} from c19. So j can be 3 or 0. Both are allowed with i=0: (0,3) allowed, (0,0) allowed. So both possible.\n\nThus i=0, j ∈ {3,0}.\n\nThus i=0 => f must be 1 (since i=0 only when f=1). So f=1. Then g=3, h=2, e=0 as before. So we have f=1, i=0.\n\nThus we have determined f=1, i=0.\n\nNow we need to find k,l consistent with j.\n\nNow we have j ∈ {3,0}. Let's explore both possibilities.\n\nCase 1: j=3.\n\nThen c9: (i,j) = (0,3) allowed (yes). c19: (j,m) = (3,0) allowed (yes). So j=3 works.\n\nNow we need to find k,l such that:\n\nc10: (j,k) = (3,k) must be allowed. c10 allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,2), (1,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2). So (3,k) allowed when k=0 (pair (3,0)) or k=2 (pair (3,2)). So k ∈ {0,2}.\n\nc11: (k,l) must be allowed. c11 allowed pairs: (0,2), (1,3), (3,3), (2,0), (3,2), (1,0). So for each possible k, we need l.\n\nc12: (l,i) = (l,0) must be allowed. c12 allowed pairs: (2,0), (1,1), (3,3), (2,3), (2,2), (3,1). So (l,0) appears only in (2,0) => l=2. So l must be 2.\n\nThus l=2.\n\nNow check c12: (l,i) = (2,0) allowed (yes). Good.\n\nNow we need to ensure c11: (k,l) = (k,2) allowed. c11 allowed pairs include (0,2) => k=0 works; (3,2) => k=3 works; also (??) (??) Actually (3,2) is allowed. Also (??) (??) (??) (??) (??). Let's list all c11 pairs: (0,2), (1,3), (3,3), (2,0), (3,2), (1,0). So (k,2) allowed for k=0 (0,2) and k=3 (3,2). So k can be 0 or 3. But earlier from c10, k ∈ {0,2}. Intersection gives k=0. So k must be 0.\n\nThus k=0, l=2.\n\nNow check c11: (0,2) allowed (yes). c10: (3,0) allowed (yes). So case j=3 yields a consistent assignment: i=0, j=3, k=0, l=2.\n\nNow we need to satisfy Cycle D: variables m,n,o,p with constraints c13,c14,c15,c16. Known m=0, n=1. Need to find o,p